WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.070
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:09.722
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.022 --> 00:00:17.597
Unit three: Philosophical Essays. Today you will concede a point while keeping your position with although, albeit and whereas.

4
00:00:17.897 --> 00:00:20.514
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:24.932
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.783 --> 00:00:26.657
Argument.

7
00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:31.229
Argument means a series of reasons that support a conclusion.

8
00:00:31.817 --> 00:00:33.979
Her argument moves step by step.

9
00:00:34.583 --> 00:00:36.806
Your turn. Say: Argument.

10
00:00:39.667 --> 00:00:40.691
Argument.

11
00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:42.381
Premise.

12
00:00:43.383 --> 00:00:46.416
Premise means a statement that an argument takes as true.

13
00:00:47.017 --> 00:00:50.200
If the premise is false, the argument fails.

14
00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:53.353
Your turn. Say: Premise.

15
00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:57.085
Premise.

16
00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:59.362
Logical fallacy.

17
00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:04.555
Logical fallacy means an error in reasoning that makes an argument invalid.

18
00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.893
Attacking the speaker is a logical fallacy.

19
00:01:08.483 --> 00:01:11.763
Your turn. Say: Logical fallacy.

20
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:16.089
Logical fallacy.

21
00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:17.990
Counterargument.

22
00:01:18.983 --> 00:01:21.796
Counterargument means a reason that opposes a position.

23
00:01:22.383 --> 00:01:25.091
A fair essay answers the strongest counterargument.

24
00:01:25.683 --> 00:01:28.939
Your turn. Say: Counterargument.

25
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:32.952
Counterargument.

26
00:01:33.717 --> 00:01:34.985
Thought experiment.

27
00:01:35.983 --> 00:01:39.597
Thought experiment means an imagined situation used to test an idea.

28
00:01:40.183 --> 00:01:43.065
The thought experiment imagined a city without laws.

29
00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:46.453
Your turn. Say: Thought experiment.

30
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:50.557
Thought experiment.

31
00:01:51.317 --> 00:01:52.840
Abstract concept.

32
00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:58.462
Abstract concept means an idea such as justice that cannot be touched or seen.

33
00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:01.235
Freedom is an abstract concept.

34
00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:04.816
Your turn. Say: Abstract concept.

35
00:02:07.667 --> 00:02:09.562
Abstract concept.

36
00:02:10.317 --> 00:02:11.260
Reasoning.

37
00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:16.235
Reasoning means the process of thinking logically to reach a conclusion.

38
00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:19.442
Clear reasoning convinces careful readers.

39
00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:22.342
Your turn. Say: Reasoning.

40
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.282
Reasoning.

41
00:02:27.017 --> 00:02:28.041
Conclusion.

42
00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:32.315
Conclusion means the final point that follows from the argument.

43
00:02:32.917 --> 00:02:35.520
The conclusion follows from both premises.

44
00:02:36.117 --> 00:02:38.711
Your turn. Say: Conclusion.

45
00:02:41.567 --> 00:02:42.626
Conclusion.

46
00:02:43.433 --> 00:02:46.991
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:53.974
Visal, a philosopher writes 'Although the argument is clever, it rests on a weak premise.' What does although do?

48
00:02:55.083 --> 00:03:04.751
It concedes a point. Although introduces the part that is true, and the main clause gives your real position. The reader sees you were fair before you disagreed.

49
00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:07.641
Are there more formal choices?

50
00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:24.179
Yes. Albeit means although it is: 'a brilliant, albeit flawed, argument'. Whereas contrasts two facts: 'Plato trusted reason, whereas Hume trusted experience.'

51
00:03:25.283 --> 00:03:26.842
What about punctuation?

52
00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:38.941
A comma separates a leading although-clause from the main clause. Albeit is not followed by a full clause, only a short phrase. Do not use but together with although.

53
00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:41.740
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:42.583 --> 00:03:46.115
Grammar focus: Concession: although and albeit and whereas.

55
00:03:46.415 --> 00:03:47.556
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:51.399
Although the argument is clever, it rests on a weak premise.

57
00:03:52.283 --> 00:03:55.560
Her case is persuasive, albeit slightly incomplete.

58
00:03:56.450 --> 00:04:00.272
Plato trusted reason, whereas Hume trusted experience.

59
00:04:01.233 --> 00:04:10.213
Although + clause concedes a point; albeit + short phrase means although it is; whereas contrasts two facts. Never use although and but together.

60
00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:15.019
Be careful! Don't say: Although it was late, but we kept reading.

61
00:04:16.967 --> 00:04:19.628
Say: Although it was late, we kept reading.

62
00:04:19.928 --> 00:04:21.974
Do not use although and but together.

63
00:04:22.767 --> 00:04:25.663
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:26.467 --> 00:04:27.979
Fair to the Other Side.

65
00:04:28.279 --> 00:04:32.925
A philosophical essay presents an argument and tests it against the strongest counterargument.

66
00:04:33.933 --> 00:04:38.812
Although a premise may seem obvious, careful reasoning asks whether it is really true.

67
00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:44.923
A logical fallacy, albeit a tempting one, can make a weak conclusion look strong.

68
00:04:45.933 --> 00:04:51.636
Some thinkers use a thought experiment, whereas others prefer to examine an abstract concept directly.

69
00:04:52.633 --> 00:04:56.816
Whichever method is used, the conclusion must follow from the premises.

70
00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:01.844
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:02.683 --> 00:05:05.194
blank the idea is old, it still matters.

72
00:05:05.494 --> 00:05:08.950
A, Although. B, Despite. C, However.

73
00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:14.093
The answer is A: Although.

74
00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:19.339
An imagined situation used to test an idea is a thought blank.

75
00:05:19.639 --> 00:05:23.293
A, premise. B, experiment. C, fallacy.

76
00:05:26.833 --> 00:05:28.972
The answer is B: experiment.

77
00:05:30.117 --> 00:05:31.663
Which sentence is correct?

78
00:05:31.963 --> 00:05:38.264
A, Although it rained, but we left.. B, Although it rained, we left.. C, Although it rained we but left..

79
00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:44.659
The answer is B: Although it rained, we left..

80
00:05:45.817 --> 00:05:48.617
A statement that an argument takes as true is a blank.

81
00:05:48.917 --> 00:05:53.163
A, conclusion. B, counterargument. C, premise.

82
00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:58.920
The answer is C: premise.

83
00:06:00.083 --> 00:06:03.813
According to the reading, what must follow from the premises?

84
00:06:04.113 --> 00:06:08.788
A, the conclusion. B, the abstract. C, the thought experiment.

85
00:06:12.333 --> 00:06:14.832
The answer is A: the conclusion.

86
00:06:16.033 --> 00:06:19.382
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:20.217 --> 00:06:24.120
Why might a philosopher use a thought experiment to explain an idea?

88
00:06:32.917 --> 00:06:34.487
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:34.787 --> 00:07:03.042
A thought experiment lets a philosopher test an idea in a situation that cannot be created in real life. By imagining an extreme or simple case, the reader can see the premise clearly and judge whether the reasoning really works. Although it is not scientific proof, it makes an abstract concept concrete and can reveal hidden problems in an argument before we accept the conclusion.

90
00:07:03.342 --> 00:07:05.086
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:05.983 --> 00:07:11.794
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and concession: although and albeit and whereas.

92
00:07:12.094 --> 00:07:17.114
Today's value is logical rigor. Follow your reasoning wherever it honestly leads.

93
00:07:17.414 --> 00:07:20.009
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

94
00:07:20.309 --> 00:07:22.984
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

